Client joins zones from the start screen (NAME/ZONE fields, JOIN, Tab focus); two browser clients verified seeing each other. Background tabs throttle rAF, so test pages need bringToFront.                                                                                                   
Zone serves wss (tls_cert/tls_key); head-of-line cost measured: harmless at 40ms RTT, ~1 hitch/1.4s at 150ms+3% loss, bridged by coasting through the shared sim.                                                                                                                              
Clicks were never broken (sub-frame presses are dropped; hold ~90ms in tests), and names are now generated call signs rather than typed.                                                                                                                                                       
Map tiles are typed classes with a variant nibble, and maps load from RLE files the server sends to clients.                                                                                                                                                                                   
Two web-client traps: a stray 'nd' broke every build while 'build.sh | tail -1' hid the exit code, shipping 3 stale bundles (start screen now prints the commit); and Defold resumes audio only from a canvas pointer, so single_file.py unlocks on any gesture.                               
Bombs now splash on wall and direct hits alike (not timeouts, criccomini's call), golden trace regenerated; feed became a notification feed. Client CPU 40%->12% of a core: a Lua buffer-stream write is one C call per float, so vwbuf writes a whole shape per call.                         
No start screen now: land flying, escape opens a menu tree over the live arena, hull change is sim_set_ship_class (a respawn), page draws its own starfield while wasm compiles. A --fragment page needs its own viewport meta or a standalone phone lays out at 980px.                        
Zone hull changes work (C2S_SHIP, core-gated on alive+full bar, upgrades lost); the address field is gone, ZONES only. Weapons are one table model: bounce, proximity, splinter, push and stall all ship as rows, unused by the baseline zone.                                                 
Zones own their rules: settings (with the weapon tables) are packed by the core and sent after the map at join and on reload. Zone files write weapons by name; apply_config rebuilds from the baseline first, so a reload is idempotent and a deleted line reverts.                           
Tech tree: a level is a rung on a per-hull ladder of patterns; an add-on is a transform applied at fire time, per trigger, carried by the shot. Greens carry no type -- the roll happens at pickup over what the hull could ever hold, so every green is takeable.                             
Prize weights are relative and read against the hull's pool; rust corrodes only what a pilot holds, so it never punishes a fresh spawn. Charges are a pattern plus an inventory, with the ready slot in two button bits rather than in sim state.                                              
criccomini's Kinesis Freestyle2 ghosts arrows+space+turn; A/D works, and it is hardware not software. Bounty is derived from what a pilot holds, points are the victim's bounty paid to the finisher, so a fresh spawn is worth nothing.                                                       
Bots hunt by bounty/distance and chase greens when nobody is near, so practice arenas grow real bounties; projectiles retuned faster than any ship (bolt 800 px/s, shell 500, frag 400, burst 600) with lives cut to hold range.                                                               
Tuning comes from ASSS dist/conf/svs, not invention: rounds inherit ship velocity so 2000 vs MaximumSpeed 3250 is deliberate; multifire +50% energy +100% cooldown per rung; prize weights 40 stat / 25 level / 30 multi-shrapnel / 70 charge.                                                 
The original gates almost nothing per ship: charges (3 each), multifire, bouncing bullets, proximity and shrapnel are universal; its whole per-ship diff is nine settings (MaxBombs, ShrapnelMax, BombBounceCount, DoubleBarrel, EmpBomb, CloakStatus, SeeMines, BrickMax, Gravity).           
spawn_prizes (default 30) loads every spawn and flattens the bot skill gap, so calibration pins it to 0. The arena is the full 1024-tile map: a hashed 64-tile cell lattice round the old centre room, meshed by the client in a window round the camera.                                      
Two silent failures: a Rust mirror one size behind the C core is heap corruption (sim_sizeof_* guards it now), and an overflowing vec layer drops primitives, looking like flickering hulls not a slow frame (draws culled to camera; watch the heartbeat lost count).                         
Perf: snapshots halved (prize tile coords + 256-tile interest radius); sim_step is superlinear in weapons x ships and lost a third to spec caching and a compact hull array. Load time is already handled by the host's brotli.                                                                
HUD reworked: energy and bounty ride on your own hull, scoreboard and loadout behind one default-off 'info' toggle, hints in a menu help page, flags as pennants, and touch controls one bottom row -- guns, bombs, then a tappable pad per charge.                                            
Touch flight is a drawn d-pad, angle-sectored so the wedge between two keys holds both. The pointing stick could not express reverse (opening range with guns still on someone); a relative stick reading the same axes felt worse -- nothing to aim at.                                       
The touch stick asks for a course, not a facing: point behind you and the ship reverses, aim held. Forward/back re-arms when the thumb crosses the origin -- hysteresis was unreachable, since the hull chases the thumb and collapses the angle it reads.                                     
A thumb arcs rather than crossing the origin, so sweeps hold the rudder until it lands, timed in seconds not frames. Zone model redesigned (decisions 23-26): one arena per process, directories relay only own observations, arenas union views and pick a type when empty.                   
criccomini wants prose deslopped and concise, docs included: no em dashes, no punchy fragments, no bold-lead bullets (skill in .claude/skills/deslop). The touch stick reads a swipe against the nearest foe, who wears a reticle while pointed at.                                            
Mobile has no reverse, settled: five schemes all felt worse than the plain pointing stick, do not retry without a pad of its own. Duels removed for now: C2S_DUEL, the Duel mode, its Lua copy and Zone's arena map out, sim_map_duel is sim_map_pit. Decision 16 deferred, design kept.       
Web build in a fresh container: bob.jar only from GitHub releases (d.defold.com 404s), and bob 1.13 needs JDK 25 (class file 69) from api.adoptium.net. Debug server builds never started a zone: (i as u16)*8192 overflows at the 9th bot; release wrapped it, so nobody saw it.              
ASSS ships no per-ship balance: of 84 settings per ship only 10 differ, all capability flags. And a room is 79 KB (sim_map 1 MB is shared by pointer) at 1.6-16 us/tick, so 615-6400 rooms per core -- hosting cost is egress not compute, ~$1.56/mo per player on Fly, ~0 on Hetzner.         
Hosting is Vultr for everything (33 regions, managed Postgres, own IPs so Docker host-networking and UDP work); Fly rejected, browsers cannot header-route. ASSS had no per-arena cap: DesiredPlaying defaults to 15 and only opens another arena, so SIM_MAX_SHIPS=64 is stricter.            
SIM_MAX_SHIPS=64 is cheap to raise (255 costs 50 us/tick, 107 KB a room; move the header and MAX_SHIPS in server/src/sim.rs together). Repel is hostile only now: both push loops test team, which covers the firer, who used to be thrown at 484 px/s.                                        
Fleet design complete in docs: catalog.md (schema + validation), discovery.md (0x40 tags, JSON bodies, INTENT carries its own TTL), zones-and-arenas.md (selection algorithm, state machine, join path with teams decided by the mode), M7 build order. Room size is per-zone.                 
criccomini settled the fleet's open questions (no chat, DNS discovery, dynamic rooms under a per-zone cap, ratings and spectators deferred); M7.5 rooms on demand then landed and was verified end to end.                                                                                     
Two arena memory findings: rooms share their zone's map by Arc (107 KB a room, not 1.1 MB), and per-client outbound queues are bounded at 40 so a stalled client cannot grow the process without limit.                                                                                        
